      "Language": "rus",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To study efficacy and safety of nebivolol in patients with stable angina pectoris."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL",
          "content": "Twenty patients with stable effort angina pectoris and positive exercise test without contraindications to beta-blockers, signs of NYHA class III-IV heart failure, and obstructive lung disease. After 5-7 days of control period all patients were given nebivolol (starting dose 5 mg/day) for 8 weeks. Graded exercise tests on treadmill and patient's diaries were used for assessment of treatment efficacy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Treatment with nebivolol (5-10 mg o.d. ) was associated with significant slowing of heart rate, lowering of blood pressure, and increase of exercise duration until appearance of angina or 1 mm ST-segment depression. Nebivolol did not affect left ventricular ejection fraction, bronchial conductance, or results of biochemical tests (including lipid profile). Adverse reaction (dizziness which disappeared after lowering of a dose) was registered in 1 patient."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Nebivolol is an effective drug for the treatment of patients with stable angina."
        }
      ]},
